<Base lang="en-US" pageType="Article" title="Tradition Day vs National Gaucho Day in Argentina" description="Argentina celebrates Tradition Day on November 10 and National Gaucho Day on December 6. Learn why, with official laws and Martín Fierro references.">
<article class="comparison"><nav><a href="/en/">English edition</a> / <a href="/en/magazine/">Cultural magazine</a> / National commemorations</nav><p class="eyebrow">ARGENTINE HISTORY · CULTURE</p><h1>Are Argentina’s Tradition Day and National Gaucho Day the same holiday?</h1><p class="lead"><strong>No.</strong> Argentina observes <strong>Tradition Day on November 10</strong> and <strong>National Gaucho Day on December 6</strong>. Both are connected to José Hernández and his celebrated poem <em>Martín Fierro</em>, but they commemorate different events.</p>
<table><caption>Two commemorations compared</caption><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Tradition Day</th><th>National Gaucho Day</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th>When?</th><td>November 10</td><td>December 6</td></tr><tr><th>Why?</th><td>Birth of José Hernández in 1834</td><td>Publication of <em>El gaucho Martín Fierro</em> in 1872</td></tr><tr><th>National law</th><td>Law 21,154 (1975)</td><td>Law 24,303 (1993)</td></tr><tr><th>Main focus</th><td>Argentina's cultural traditions</td><td>The gaucho's historical and cultural legacy</td></tr></tbody></table>
<section><h2>November 10: Tradition Day</h2><p>José Hernández was born on November 10, 1834. Buenos Aires Province established the observance through Law 4,756 in 1939. National Law 21,154, enacted in 1975, established Tradition Day throughout Argentina. The date honors the traditions passed down through generations: music, stories, regional customs, crafts, and community life.</p><p><a href="https://www.infoleg.gob.ar/wp-content/uploads/2014/10/LEY-ACU-1056.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the official Spanish text of Law 21,154, including its nationwide designation and recognition of San Martín as City of Tradition ↗</a></p></section>
<section><h2>December 6: National Gaucho Day</h2><p>This date recalls the publication of Hernández's <em>El gaucho Martín Fierro</em> in 1872. Law 24,303, passed on December 15, 1993 and published on January 10, 1994, officially designated December 6 as National Gaucho Day. It recognizes the gaucho's role in Argentina's rural history and cultural identity.</p><p><a href="https://www.argentina.gob.ar/normativa/nacional/norma-698/texto" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the original Spanish text of Law 24,303 on Argentina's official legislation website ↗</a></p><p><a href="https://www.argentina.gob.ar/noticias/por-que-se-celebra-el-dia-nacional-del-gaucho-en-la-argentina" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the Argentine Ministry of Culture's historical explanation of the December 6 commemoration (in Spanish) ↗</a></p></section>
<section><h2>What does Martín Fierro have to do with both dates?</h2><p>Hernández's poem portrays the voice of a gaucho confronting conscription, injustice, loss and exile. Its two parts appeared in 1872 and 1879. Tradition Day recalls the author's birth and the nation's inherited customs; National Gaucho Day focuses on the gaucho and the literary milestone associated with the poem. They are complementary, not interchangeable.</p><p><a href="/en/reader/">Explore the original poem and its reading guide →</a></p><p><a href="/en/what-is-a-gaucho/">Learn about the historical gaucho and Argentine rural culture →</a></p></section>
<section><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Are these national public holidays?</h3><p>A nationally recognized commemorative day is not automatically a statutory day off. Cultural observances and public holidays are different legal categories.</p><h3>Which date should I use for a school project?</h3><p>Use November 10 for Tradition Day and José Hernández's birthday; use December 6 for National Gaucho Day and the commemoration associated with the 1872 poem.</p></section>
<p class="note">Primary sources: Argentine Laws 21,154 and 24,303, linked above. English explanations are editorial summaries, not official translations.</p></article>
